          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c is 58.",
          "answer": "I can't tell you what an HbA1c of 58 means from the guidance I retrieved. The passage that would have given the diagnostic thresholds for HbA1c (the \"type 2 diabetes screening\" guideline) was cut off before it stated the numbers. The part I could read says that when two different tests are done and only one is abnormal, the usual approach is to repeat the abnormal one. That passage doesn't give a number for 58.\n\nThe retrieved guidance does say how HbA1c is used once someone is being treated for diabetes. The \"type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals\" guideline explains that HbA1c reflects average blood sugar over roughly the past three months. It says people not at their goal, or whose treatment has recently changed, are usually tested every three months. People who are at goal on a stable treatment are usually tested at least twice a year. It also describes a less strict goal, below 8 percent, for some people, but it doesn't say whether that applies to you.\n\nPlease take this result to a doctor or other clinician. They can tell you what 58 means for you, whether the test needs repeating, and what follow-up makes sense. If you have symptoms such as extreme thirst, frequent urination, blurred vision, unexplained weight loss, or feeling very unwell, seek care promptly rather than waiting for an appointment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
