          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is that high?",
          "answer": "The guidance I retrieved doesn't say whether 58 is high. None of the passages gives the diagnostic cut-off for HbA1c, and the search output was partly cut off, so I can't fill that gap.\n\nWhat I can share is from the \"type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals\" guideline. It says an HbA1c below 7 percent is a reasonable goal for many non-pregnant adults. For some people a stricter goal, below 6.5 percent, may be appropriate, and for others a less strict goal may be set. These are targets for people already being treated, not tests for diagnosis, so they don't tell you whether your result is above or below a diagnostic line.\n\nAlso check the units on your report. HbA1c is reported as a percentage in some places and in mmol/mol in others. A number like 58 can mean very different things depending on the unit, and the retrieved guidance doesn't explain how to convert between them.\n\nPlease ask the clinician who ordered the test what your result means, what units it uses, and whether it needs repeating.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
