          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What code would that be recorded under?",
          "answer": "The retrieved coding guidance doesn't name a specific code for an HbA1c result, so I can't tell you which code your record would use.\n\nWhat it does say:\n\n- The \"Coding type 2 diabetes\" passage says abnormal glucose findings that fall short of a diabetes diagnosis are not coded to E11.\n- The \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings\" passage says elevated glucose levels, including prediabetes, sit inside the blood-findings part of that chapter. The excerpt is cut off, so I can't confirm the exact code range from it.\n- The \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 4\" passage says an isolated raised or lowered glucose result, with no diagnosis recorded, is classified in Chapter 18, not in the diabetes block.\n\nWhich code is used depends on whether a clinician has recorded a diagnosis, and that decision belongs to the clinician or medical coder. Your clinician or the lab report can tell you what was recorded.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
